00:17:24.638 --> 00:17:25.471
That's tense.

00:17:25.471 --> 00:17:28.530
So earlier in the season, we
saw that up until this point,

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.900
Annie had largely been
motivated by a hatred

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.360
towards her adoptive father,

00:17:33.360 --> 00:17:35.490
which evolved into a love for him

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:37.440
and a desire to rescue
him from the rumbling.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:40.056
Now here, after realizing
her motivating aim

00:17:40.056 --> 00:17:42.290
of saving her father
is no longer possible,

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.550
she seems like she kind
of wants to like give up.

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.088
But she recommits to the cause

00:17:46.088 --> 00:17:48.760
because it's the right thing to do

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.020
even though it's very hard.

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.190
And there's an underlying melancholy here,

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:55.900
that's clear the odds
are very, very very much

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:57.810
against our gang right here.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.420
But this right here brings
up another aspect of stoicism

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.735
which is the point of pursuing virtue

00:18:02.735 --> 00:18:06.342
isn't to simply bring
about a desirable result.

00:18:06.342 --> 00:18:09.760
The point is to act in
accordance with universal nature,

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:14.300
i.e. a sense of natural justice,
which will make you happy,

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:16.910
even if you don't actually get
the thing you were aiming at.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:18.500
In this case, it seems clear that even

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.182
if our mini resistance
is destined to fail,

00:18:21.182 --> 00:18:23.774
they will still experience
a type of happiness

00:18:23.774 --> 00:18:26.400
in continuing to do what's right.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.370
It's not dissimilar from
Immanuel Kant moral philosophy

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:32.496
in which right intention
matters as much, if not more,

00:18:32.496 --> 00:18:34.350
than the right result.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:37.330
As the season ends with
Eren's fInding Titan,

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:40.230
and his Titan army, stomping
out human civilization,

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:42.580
it at least seems like the best our gang

00:18:42.580 --> 00:18:45.560
of resistors can hope for
is doing what's virtuous

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:46.960
even if it doesn't work out.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:48.760
And this relates to
another part of stoicism

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.580
namely that it's not only
about learning how to live,

00:18:51.580 --> 00:18:53.760
it's about learning how to die as well.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.196
Seneca here, plagiarized
by Montaigne said,

00:18:56.196 --> 00:19:00.550
"He who has learned to die
has unlearned how to serve."

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:01.787
And his Epic Titus said,

00:19:01.787 --> 00:19:04.322
"Keep death before your eyes every day

00:19:04.322 --> 00:19:07.110
and then you will never
have any object thought

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:08.451
or any excessive desire."

00:19:08.451 --> 00:19:10.899
All of this according to Haddo,

00:19:10.899 --> 00:19:12.887
it means that we should remain present

00:19:12.887 --> 00:19:17.887
as if every moment is paradoxically
our first and our last.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:19.063
Okay.

00:19:19.063 --> 00:19:21.710
So does part two of season
four of "Attack On Titan",

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.424
give us a masterclass
on how to become Stoics?

00:19:24.424 --> 00:19:25.390
Not really,

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.750
but much of the season
shows us what can happen

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.450
when hatred, anger and fear take priority

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:33.510
over logical deliberation
in the pursuit of virtue.

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:35.620
This helps us understand
what the stoic saw

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:38.690
as an undesirable and
unvirtuous way to live.

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:39.820
And towards the end of the season,

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.530
we start to see how moving
beyond negative passions

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:43.930
and emotions to approach things

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:45.680
from a more logical perspective,

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:48.440
leads a group of characters
to overcome their hatred

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:50.200
for the sake of the common good.

00:19:50.200 --> 00:19:52.641
And this gets at coolest
thing about stoicism.

00:19:52.641 --> 00:19:56.210
It's really a philosophy of life.

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:58.840
The stoics weren't
primarily focused on (beep)

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:01.930
like making metaphysical arguments
about the nature of being

00:20:01.930 --> 00:20:04.752
or logically deliberating
on the nature of truth,

00:20:04.752 --> 00:20:07.670
they think philosophically for the sake

00:20:07.670 --> 00:20:08.870
of living a good life,

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.680
something that puts them in touch

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.360
with the spirit of Socrates.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:13.560
It also makes them predecessors

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.750
of the 19th and 20th century
existential philosophers,

00:20:16.750 --> 00:20:19.020
from who philosophy is primarily about

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.804
how we live life and make
sense of our existence

00:20:21.804 --> 00:20:24.720
and not merely about
how we understand things

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.028
in a detached sense.

00:20:26.028 --> 00:20:27.590
And if you're interested in learning more

00:20:27.590 --> 00:20:28.423
about this tradition,

00:20:28.423 --> 00:20:30.597
I can't recommend Pierre Hadot's

00:20:30.597 --> 00:20:33.240
"Philosophy As a Way
of Life" highly enough.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:36.550
So access your library card
Titan and check it out.

